{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790979957,"nonce":"KtyJ3vEaNjmSwCk74cwEMFDzBLQ=","title":"ASH_PROFILE_THREADS=all signals non-threads on macOS arm64 and samples nothing","message":"`hlp_gc_registered_threads` hands the profiler each mutator's GC identity, and the sampler passes those to `pthread_kill`. On macOS arm64 that identity is `TPIDRRO_EL0 \u0026 !7` (`thread_self_fast` in std/src/gc.rs), the thread's TSD base, not its `pthread_t`. The TSD base sits 224 bytes past `pthread_self()` and `pthread_kill` on it returns ESRCH (3), so once the thread list resolves no thread is signalled and no samples land.\n\nReproducer: a Haxe program with a worker thread burning 2s beside main.\n\n```\nASH_PROFILE=sample ASH_PROFILE_THREADS=main ash --mode interp spin.hl  -\u003e 1637 samples\nASH_PROFILE=sample ASH_PROFILE_THREADS=all  ash --mode interp spin.hl  -\u003e 64 samples\n```\n\nThe 64 are the ticks before the thread list resolves (the sampler looks it up on its refresh ticks, every 64th). Checked in C: `pthread_self=0x1f8c5e180 tsd=0x1f8c5e260 kill(tsd)=3`.\n\nLinux is unaffected: `thread_self_fast` there is `pthread_self`.\n\nFix direction: record the real `pthread_t` in the mutator record at registration and export that from `hlp_gc_registered_threads`, rather than converting the TSD base with libpthread's private offset.","files":null}]}